- Steve Ruiz (@steveruizok, tldraw), 2026-10-31: good prompt "find all of the implicit state machines in my app" (12.5K bookmarks). Follow-up 2026-11-02 'Guess which release came after this prompt' with engine perf charts (causal claim implied, not evidenced in the thread). Jamon Holmgren's reply shows the expanded version: subagents spidering a game codebase in parallel, evaluate findings, return list + doc. Connection: phcc's design already derives its four state machines explicitly (Architecture chat, 2026-11-07); the Ruiz prompt is the general hunt for implicit ones. Offered to Will: run the hunt on phext-lattice, SQ, or libphext-rs.
# Rename: 'Doomer Support System' retired — audience naming correction

Coordinate: 18.7.11/9.6.10/6.6.9
Issued: 2026-11-07 21:33 CST
Request (verbatim): you can't say "Doomer Support System" to someone who cares about human flourishing and skynet
Status: completed — Will chose 'Alignment Support System'; rename applied everywhere public-facing

Notes:
- Parent: 26.5.7/7.5.9/5.5.8. Reasons the name fails, recorded: (1) it labels the user a 'doomer' — a dismissal in tech discourse — to exactly the safety/flourishing audience the product serves; (2) it collides with Will's own Doomer.phext vocabulary, where Doomer/Paver is the negative register for safety-motivated opponents ('No Skynet!'); (3) it pitches doom (giving up) for a product whose stance is flourishing + alignment.
- Collision checks 2026-11-07: 'Sentient Support System' — no product with the exact phrase found ('Sentient' alone is crowded: Sentient OS, Sentient AGI/SENT, Sentient360 ITSM). 'Alignment Support System' — no AI product with the exact phrase found. 'Flourish*' — crowded in AI wellbeing (Flourish Science app), avoid as brand word.

Results:
- Renamed 2026-11-07 21:34 CST: page (title, header, footer, report string), brief (title + Name section recording the retirement and its reasons; audience wording 'doomer' also removed), README, and MEMORY lead. Directory/file paths keep the retired name as stable internal paths, noted as legacy in brief and README. Page re-verified after rename (see parent task results).
