# Build an AST Layer 2 format on phext; collect every AST into one phext organized by 6D UML (wbic16/every-ast)

Coordinate: 26.6.9/9.7.12/6.6.9
Issued: 2026-11-08 01:20 CST
Request (verbatim): make a note to build an AST Layer 2 format on phext that mogs current approaches — see https://x.com/_Felipe/status/2119248191564427610 — this will look like a joke, but... we're going to collect every AST out there into one phext, organized using 6D UML — https://github.com/wbic16/every-ast
Status: noted 2026-11-08 — build not yet directed

Notes:
- Framing (Will): a Layer 2 format on phext (composition lives outside the scroll — Layer 2 formats implement things like databases, per Will 2026-10-30) whose subject is ASTs, designed to beat ("mog") current AST representations/serializations.
- Scope: collect every AST out there into ONE phext, organized using 6D UML. Candidate corpus: the AST node vocabularies/schemas of every language and tool — compiler front-ends, tree-sitter grammars, Babel/ESTree, LLVM, GCC, Roslyn, etc. (enumeration is build-time work).
- 6D UML already exists in phext-lattice src/uml6d.rs: the six inner dimensions carry SDLC semantics (Collection=phase, Volume=sprint/release, Book=module, Chapter=component, Section=file, Scroll=unit); an artifact's 5D address is invariant across phases, so traceability is a coordinate walk. The AST corpus organization inherits this mapping.
- Target repo wbic16/every-ast verified 2026-11-08: exists, currently EMPTY — a named home waiting for its first commit.
- Reference post: https://x.com/_Felipe/status/2119248191564427610 (@_Felipe) — cited by Will as the "see:" for what current approaches do; its text is being fetched and will be appended when read.
- Reference post read 2026-11-08 (browser fetch): Felipe O. Carvalho (@_Felipe): "Saying this again: programming language syntax is the best serialization format for syntax tree. It's human readable, it's compact and, if well-designed it's easy to parse." Quoted in it, Kit Langton (@kitlangton): "We'll be liberal in what we accept. Because blood is stored in abstract tree form, syntax is flexible. Even if we have one canonical form, we can accept a wide range of inputs." — i.e., the incumbent position the format must beat: concrete syntax IS the serialization, with the tree as the stored form underneath.
# Slice 6: linker resolution state machine (tccelf.c)

Coordinate: 27.4.7/5.5.11/6.4.8
Issued: 2026-11-07
Request (from session record): continue: linker machine
Status: Complete 2026-11-07.

Notes:
- Slice 6 of the phcc modernization (design A.3 machine #3, Book 5, tccelf.c), built additively on the consolidated base 646144c7... in ~/workspace/phcc-slice6/; parent merges, nothing pushed.
- Machine at Design 1.1.1/3.2.5/1.2.2: 8 states / 19 events / 68 transitions / 11 invariants, notes N1-N12; merge heart is set_elf_sym's branch order (tccelf.c:722-779); resolution is a separate sweep (relocate_syms, 3 modes), not a load pass (N8).
- Unresolved observation, not claimed: exe-path common size pilots disagreed (bind_exe_dynsyms SHN_COMMON/dynsym handling suspected, tccelf.c:2081-2127); first-common-wins (N6) is proven only at the merged table via tcc -r readelf sizes.

Results:
- Static: PASS, 0 violations; events resolved 19/19 against Vol 3 tccelf.c members (tools/check_linker.py).
- Dynamic: authored 19/19 PASS (independent model == ranch phcc; gcc oracle agrees except L15a — GNU ld errors where this linker ignores a hidden presentation after a definition, scroll note N12); tests2 corpus 10/10 PASS under -run (125_atomic_misc excluded: Makefile -dt protocol; 34_array_assignment excluded: Makefile SKIP list).
- Exit test: PASS — seeded goto do_patch in set_elf_sym's SHN_UNDEF branch caught by exactly the 14 model-derived UNDEF-over-definition scenarios; violated transition named (DEFINED --ev_set [shndx == SHN_UNDEF...]--> DEFINED); corpus fell to 1/10 under the same row (81_types match vacuous, empty .expect).
- Regressions: 5, Changelog-sourced (0.9.24 visibility + invalid relocation entry, 0.9.22 definitions fixes, 0.9.16 COMMON, 0.9.11 symbol+offset) at Regressions 1.1.1/6.2.5/1.2.1-5.
- Gate v6: base regression clean (v5 tables byte-identical + machine-#3-absent block only); verdict Meta Sec 14 (b77debbb...), register Meta Sec 15, binding CURRENT; verdict reproducible byte-identically.
- Output: ~/workspace/phcc-slice6/phcc.phext, 54,412,105 B, SHA-256 40286bd44629713e5b6db2f735c97e98571ea3575dd6d426b93e8b1d3a43b909; base scrolls changed: 0; additions: 28 content scrolls + 2 zero-byte nav slots. REPORT.md in the slice dir.
# TOAPDD — The Art of Phext Driven Development: Top 20 Knuth passages

Coordinate: 27.5.8/7.6.10/6.6.8
Issued: 2026-11-07 00:05 CST
Request (verbatim): TOAPDD - The Art of Phext Driven Development

I have copies of Knuth's works. I need to iterate through all of his examples and identify which ones are phext-friendly. Can we improve upon his lessons with the benefit of hindsight? Give me a list of the top 20 passages to focus on initially.
Status: completed — Top 20 list delivered; full-corpus A/B/C/D iteration is the open follow-on

Notes:
- No Knuth texts found in workspace; Will holds copies. List will be addressed by TAOCP volume/section/algorithm so it can be located in any edition.
- Locators verified against Knuth Stanford pages + publisher TOCs; corrections applied: GC is 2.3.5 not 2.5, B-trees inside 6.2.4, backtracking is 4B 7.2.2, Dancing Links 7.2.2.1, Algorithm X name collision (4.5.2 extended Euclid vs 7.2.2.1 exact cover) flagged.
- Flags carried in deliverable: Vol.2 3.5 title unverified; 5.2/6.4 algorithm letters not item-verified; 2.4 has no numbered subsections.

Results:
- Top 20 list: ~/workspace/your_files/toapdd-top-20-knuth-passages.md — 3 tiers (Build on 1-12, Reframe 13-17, Read as warning 18-20), Class D refusals, suggested working order 16>1>2>4>3.
